<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">East India Company</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as English East India Company</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Company was an English, later British, chartered trading company founded in 1600. Beginning as a trading concern, it became a military and governing power in India, defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> and reducing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> to a pensioner, until the Crown took over in 1858. It was the main instrument of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, French and other European companies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-india-company.jpg" alt="East India Company" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yaddah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_British_East_India_Company_(1801).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Mughal Empire from which it never recovered: the loss of the imperial treasury undermined the financial foundations of Mughal authority, accelerated the empire's fragmentation into competing successor states, and helped create the power vacuum that the <b>British East India Company</b> and other European companies would eventually fill.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In two great waves the rebels seized the capital, and King Gaurinath Singha fled to Guwahati; at his request the <b>British East India Company</b> sent Captain Thomas Welsh with a small force in 1792, which restored him before being recalled in 1794.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's early decades were marked by intense commercial rivalry with the Dutch Republic — whose <b>East India Company</b> (VOC) long outperformed its English counterpart — and by the gradual development of the fiscal-military state that would underpin British global power: the Navigation Acts (from 1651), the Bank…</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Since the mid-eighteenth century the <b>East India Company</b> had conquered most of the subcontinent, from Bengal after the Battle of Plassey in 1757 to the Punjab, annexed from the Sikh Empire in 1849.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The treaty Shuja Shah signed with the British <b>East India Company</b> on 7 June 1809, the first Afghan pact with a European power, pledged joint action against Franco-Persian aggression and foreshadowed the British restoration of Shuja in 1839 and the First Anglo-Afghan War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Although the <b>English East India Company</b> had been chartered two years before the VOC, the Dutch company's permanent capital, tradable shares and sovereign powers made it something new, enabling a republic of perhaps 1.5 to 2 million people to project power across four continents within half a century…</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
